# ANV-0057 -- the seeded-mutation drill had no null control, and five of its
# nine rows were scoring the harness's own failure as coverage
#
# The drill reported "detected 6/7 deliberate bugs". Adding one row -- a
# mutation that changes nothing -- showed the real number was 2/7.
seed        = 1 and 7 (bank, snapshot isolation), the two seeds ANV-0056 fails
              on. That is the entire finding: those two seeds are what five
              rows were detecting.
workload    = txn_bank, kBank and kListAppend
reproduce   = build the tree at 95318de with one extra row in the mutation
              table of test/txn_faults.cc:

                {"no mutation", Expectation::kControl, txn::Level::kSnapshot,
                 [](workloads::TxnBankConfig*) {}, "the noise floor"},

              and run `anvil_txn_faults 30`.
expect      = the null row fires 2/10, on seeds 1 and 7 -- and so do
              `no refresh on push`, `uncertain reads never restart`, `terminal
              status is not final`, `uncertainty never honoured` and the
              `parallel commit` control, on seeds 1 and 7 and nothing else.
              `lost update` fires on {1,6,7,8,9} and `intents invisible to
              readers` on {6,9,10}: those two are the only rows carrying
              information about their own mutation.

              After the fix the drill prints, for every row, the seeds it fired
              on *that its cell's null control did not*, and asserts on that
              number rather than on the raw count.
three separate defects, one row:
  1. no null control at all, so a row's detection count was never compared
     against the harness's own failure rate.
  2. `uncertain reads never restart` and `uncertainty never honoured` ran at
     kSnapshot, where base_profile selects the oracle and Coordinator::begin
     sets `uncertainty_limit = start` -- "with the oracle there is none". The
     window is empty, VersionStore::get's uncertainty branch is unreachable,
     and neither flag had anything to switch off. Provably vacuous rows.
  3. every row ran against the bank, whose oracle is a conserved total. A
     conserved total cannot see a whole transaction disappearing (a transfer is
     balanced, so losing all of it leaves the sum right) and cannot see write
     skew (the bank writes every key it reads, which is the one shape write
     skew is not). `no refresh on push` and `terminal status is not final` were
     asking a question their workload could not answer.
note        = api_visible: no. S4: an unreliable harness invalidates every
              result after it, and this one was inflating a headline number.
